Here are 5 well-cited, recent papers highly relevant to your breast cancer ultrasound–mammogram imaging fusion work:

1. CNN-Based Cross-Modality Fusion for Enhanced Breast Cancer Detection Using Mammography and Ultrasound
Source: PubMed Central / MDPI (2024) Why it's relevant: Directly addresses cross-modality fusion of mammography and ultrasound using CNN architectures. Achieves 92.3% accuracy on mammography and 94.3% on fused data, outperforming single-modality models. Uses public datasets (RSNA, Kaggle). Excellent methodological reference for fusion pipeline design. Link: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11679931/

2. Multimodal Classification of Breast Cancer Using Feature Level Fusion of Mammogram and Ultrasound Images in Machine Learning Paradigm
Authors: Atrey, K., Singh, B.K. & Bodhey, N.K. Journal: Multimedia Tools and Applications, Springer (2024) Why it's relevant: Proposes a feature-level fusion approach (42 grayscale features) with SVM classifier. Achieves 98.84% accuracy with fused modalities vs. 93.41% (mammogram alone) and 91.67% (ultrasound alone). Strong justification for multimodal over single-modal CAD. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1007/s11042-023-16414-6

3. A Deep Learning-Based Multimodal Medical Imaging Model for Breast Cancer Screening
Journal: Scientific Reports, Nature (2025) Why it's relevant: Uses 790 patients with 2,235 mammography + 1,348 ultrasound images across six DL models. Reports specificity of 96.41% for the multimodal model. Comprehensive performance comparison between multimodal and single-modal approaches using AUC, sensitivity, specificity, and precision. Link: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-99535-2

4. Integration of Ultrasound and Mammogram for Multimodal Classification of Breast Cancer Using Hybrid Residual Neural Network and Machine Learning
Journal: Image and Vision Computing, ScienceDirect (2024) Why it's relevant: Proposes a hybrid ResNet + machine learning architecture specifically combining US and mammogram. Covers both feature extraction and decision-level fusion strategies, making it directly applicable to your methodology design. Link: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S026288562400091X

5. Deep Learning in Breast Cancer Imaging: A Decade of Progress and Future Directions
Source: IEEE Reviews in Biomedical Engineering / arXiv (2024) Why it's relevant: A comprehensive survey of 366 papers (2012–2022) covering mammography, ultrasound, MRI, and pathology. Includes a dedicated section on multimodal learning and fusion strategies. Essential for situating your work within the broader literature and identifying research gaps. Link: https://arxiv.org/html/2304.06662v4

Quick tip for your literature review: These papers collectively cover the three main fusion levels — pixel/image-level, feature-level, and decision-level fusion — so you can use them to frame your methodology choice. Papers 1 and 3 are great for motivation and results benchmarking, Paper 2 for feature-fusion methodology, Paper 4 for architecture design, and Paper 5 as your broad survey anchor.

