Based on my exhaustive search across multiple queries, here is the honest answer:

Papers Using the BCMID Dataset — Exhaustive List
The BCMID (Breast Cancer Multimodal Imaging Dataset) is a very recently released dataset, published on Zenodo in March 2025, containing ultrasound and mammogram images for 323 adult female patients, sourced from Ayady Almostakbal Hospital in Alexandria, Egypt. 
Frontiers
 Because it is so new and the files are access-restricted, adoption by the broader research community has been extremely limited.

To date, I have found only one published paper that explicitly cites and uses BCMID:

1. ✅ A Comparative Analysis of Federated Learning for Multi-Class Breast Cancer Classification in Ultrasound Imaging
Authors: Noha S. Tawfik, Noha Ghatwary, et al. (Arab Academy for Science, Technology and Maritime Transport / University of Lincoln) Journal: AI (MDPI), 2025, Vol. 6(12), Article 316 DOI: https://doi.org/10.3390/ai6120316 Published: December 4, 2025

How BCMID was used: The paper uses BCMID as one of three federated learning client datasets (alongside BUSI and BUS-UCLM), incorporating only the ultrasound scans from BCMID's 323 patients for multi-class breast cancer classification. The authors applied FedAvg and FedProx aggregation strategies and reported that FL models achieved up to 8% higher accuracy compared to local and centralized training. 
MDPI

Notable: This paper is co-authored by the same group (Tawfik, Ghatwary) that created the BCMID dataset itself, making it effectively a companion paper to the dataset.

⚠️ Important Caveat for Your Work
There are currently no other peer-reviewed papers found in the literature that independently used BCMID for multimodal image fusion or any other task. This has two implications for your research:

This is actually an opportunity. If you use BCMID for ultrasound–mammogram fusion, you would be among the first researchers to exploit the dataset's core multimodal design (the paired US + mammogram per patient), which is precisely what it was built for but has not yet been used for in any published study. This is a strong novelty and contribution argument for your paper.

Practical note: Since the dataset files are restricted on Zenodo, you will need to contact the authors (Noha Seddik Tawfik / Noha Ghatwary at the Arab Academy for Science, Technology and Maritime Transport) directly to request access.

