📌 Gap Addressed
Reviews highlight that multi-imaging fusion more closely mirrors how radiologists integrate diverse visual information, but practical challenges remain including heterogeneous imaging protocols, scanner variability, and incomplete modality availability across real-world cohorts. 
PubMed Central
 Crucially, pixel-level spatial image fusion of US and mammogram remains almost entirely unexplored — all existing works fuse at the feature or decision level, bypassing the complementary spatial texture information locked in each modality's raw pixel space.

🎯 Problem Statement
Develop an end-to-end conditional diffusion model that synthesizes a photorealistic pseudo-mammogram from an ultrasound image (and vice versa), then fuses the real + synthetic image pair for downstream malignancy classification — enabling deployment in settings where only one modality is available.

➡️ Input
Training: Paired US + Mammogram per patient (BCMID, BCDR)
Inference: Single modality only — either US OR Mammogram
⬅️ Output
Synthesized counterpart image (US→synthetic Mammo OR Mammo→synthetic US) — pixel-level output
Fused real + synthetic feature map
Final malignancy classification + segmentation mask
FID score for synthesis quality; downstream classification gain
🏗️ Proposed Architecture
Stage 1 — Cross-Modal Synthesis: Latent Diffusion Model (LDM) conditioned on one modality to generate the other; trained with paired data (BCMID/BCDR); loss = reconstruction + perceptual + adversarial
Stage 2 — Fusion Classifier: Dual CNN on [real modality + synthesized modality] feature maps; cross-attention fusion; classification + segmentation heads
Stage 3 — Joint Fine-tuning: End-to-end gradient flow through both stages with task-aware synthesis guidance loss
📂 Datasets
BCMID (323 paired — core training)
BCDR (1,734 paired — primary benchmark)
BUSI (US only — test synthesis quality US→Mammo)
CBIS-DDSM (Mammo only — test synthesis quality Mammo→US)
📊 Evaluation Metrics
Synthesis: FID, SSIM, LPIPS | Classification: AUC, F1 | Ablation: real+real vs. real+synthetic vs. synthetic+synthetic

