🧠 Problem Statement 5
SAM2 + CLIP Adapted Foundation Model for Zero-Shot Cross-Modal Lesion Detection and Grounding in Ultrasound and Mammogram
📌 Gap Addressed
Multimodal imaging systems have shown promising ability to infer molecular breast cancer subtypes directly from noninvasive imaging using intra- and inter-modality attention mechanisms. 
PubMed Central
 However, foundation models (SAM, CLIP, LLaVA) have not been adapted for the specific challenge of cross-modal breast lesion grounding between ultrasound and mammogram, which have fundamentally different contrast mechanisms, resolution, and spatial semantics. Many data sets do not include all modalities for each patient due to high acquisition costs and fragmented storage systems between departments 
PubMed Central
 — making zero-shot and few-shot capability crucial for clinical deployment.

🎯 Problem Statement
Adapt Segment Anything Model 2 (SAM2) and CLIP as a dual foundation model backbone — using lightweight LoRA adapters per modality — to enable zero-shot and few-shot cross-modal breast lesion detection, segmentation, and malignancy grounding from paired or unpaired ultrasound and mammogram images, guided by radiological text prompts.

