🧠 Problem Statement 4
Graph-Structured Relational Transformer for Population-Level Weakly Supervised Breast Cancer Grading: Exploiting Patient Similarity Graphs Across BCMID Modalities
📌 Core Research Gap
All existing multimodal breast cancer methods treat each patient independently — a single forward pass per patient. However, BCMID's 323 patients contain rich relational structure: patients with similar BI-RADS grades have visually similar lesion appearances across both modalities. This population-level similarity is an entirely unexploited form of weak supervision. Graph Neural Networks over patient similarity graphs have shown strong semi-supervised performance in citation networks and genomics, but have never been applied to cross-modal medical image fusion where graph edges are defined by cross-modal visual similarity between patients.

🎯 Problem Statement
Construct a dynamic cross-modal patient similarity graph over all BCMID patients, where node features are dual-modality ViT embeddings and edge weights encode cross-modal visual similarity between patients. Train a Graph Transformer in a semi-supervised setting — where BI-RADS labels propagate from 20% labeled patients to 80% unlabeled patients via message passing — to predict BI-RADS grades for all patients simultaneously.

➡️ Precise Input
Step 1 — Node Feature Extraction:

For each patient P_i ∈ BCMID (i = 1...323):
  h_us^i  = ViT_encoder(I_us^i)   ∈ ℝ^{768}
  h_mm^i  = ViT_encoder(I_mm^i)   ∈ ℝ^{768}
  x_i     = Concat[h_us^i, h_mm^i] ∈ ℝ^{1536}   ← node feature

Node matrix: X ∈ ℝ^{323 × 1536}
Step 2 — Dynamic Graph Construction:

Edge weight between patient P_i and P_j:
  w_ij = α · cos_sim(h_us^i, h_us^j)    (US visual similarity)
        + β · cos_sim(h_mm^i, h_mm^j)   (Mammo visual similarity)
        + γ · cos_sim(h_us^i, h_mm^j)   (US_i vs Mammo_j — cross-modal!)
  
  where α + β + γ = 1  (learnable weights)

Sparsify: keep top-K neighbors (K=10) per node
Adjacency matrix: A ∈ ℝ^{323 × 323}  (sparse)

Example edges for Patient P_042 (BI-RADS 4):
  w_{42,117} = 0.87  (P_117 also BI-RADS 4 — visually similar lesion)
  w_{42,003} = 0.21  (P_003 BI-RADS 2 — dissimilar)
  w_{42,089} = 0.76  (P_089 BI-RADS 4 — similar morphology)
Step 3 — Semi-Supervised Labels:

Labeled set L: 65 patients (20% of 323) → BI-RADS ground truth known
Unlabeled set U: 258 patients → BI-RADS to be predicted via label propagation
⬅️ Precise Output
Output 1 — BI-RADS Predictions for ALL 323 patients:

Ŷ ∈ ℝ^{323 × 5}   (probability distribution over BI-RADS 1-5 per patient)

Patient P_042 (unlabeled during training):
  ŷ_42 = [0.02, 0.05, 0.11, 0.74, 0.08]  → BI-RADS 4 (predicted)
  
Patient P_107 (unlabeled):
  ŷ_107 = [0.61, 0.28, 0.08, 0.02, 0.01] → BI-RADS 1 (predicted, likely benign)
Output 2 — Learned Patient Similarity Graph:

Attention edge weights after training:
  A_learned ∈ ℝ^{323 × 323}  (dynamic — updated each epoch)
  
Interpretable cluster visualization:
  t-SNE of node embeddings → patients cluster by BI-RADS grade
  Community detection → sub-populations with distinct cancer subtypes
  
Cross-modal edge analysis:
  α, β, γ (learned) → e.g., α=0.3, β=0.5, γ=0.2
  → Mammo similarity is most discriminative for this patient cohort
Output 3 — Label Propagation Map:

For each unlabeled patient P_i:
  Propagated label = Σ_j∈N(i) w_ij · ŷ_j  (soft label from neighbors)
  → Shows which labeled patients "taught" each unlabeled patient
  → Clinically: "Patient P_042's lesion pattern resembles P_117, P_089 (both BI-RADS 4)"
🏗️ Architecture — Cross-Modal Graph Transformer
PHASE 1 — Node Feature Extraction:
  Frozen pretrained ViT-Small (pretrained on BCMID using CroMAE from PS1!)
  → h_us^i, h_mm^i per patient
  → x_i = Concat[h_us^i, h_mm^i] ∈ ℝ^{1536}

PHASE 2 — Dynamic Graph Construction:
  Compute pairwise similarities → A ∈ ℝ^{323×323}
  α, β, γ = learnable scalars (initialized to 1/3 each)
  Top-K sparsification with K=10

PHASE 3 — Graph Transformer Layers:
  For L=6 layers:
    h_i^{l+1} = LayerNorm(
      MultiHead_GraphAttn(h_i^l, {h_j^l | j∈N(i)}, A)
      + FFN(h_i^l)
    )
    
  Graph Attention:
    e_ij = (W_Q h_i) · (W_K h_j)^T / √d  ·  w_ij  
    (edge weight modulates attention — high-similarity neighbors get more weight)
    α_ij = softmax_j(e_ij)
    h_i^{new} = Σ_j α_ij · W_V h_j

PHASE 4 — Semi-Supervised Loss:
  L_supervised = CE(ŷ_i, y_i)  for i ∈ L (20% labeled)
  
  L_consistency = Σ_{i∈U} Σ_{j∈N(i)} w_ij · KL(ŷ_i || ŷ_j)
  (unlabeled patients should agree with their neighbors — smoothness)
  
  L_orthogonal_edges = ||A - A^T||_F  (symmetry enforcement)
  
  L_total = L_supervised + λ₁·L_consistency + λ₂·L_ortho_edges

PHASE 5 — Dynamic Graph Update (per epoch):
  Re-compute A using updated node embeddings h^l
  → Graph evolves as representations improve — progressive self-refinement
Novelty:

First Graph Transformer applied to multi-modal US+Mammogram patient similarity — treating 323 patients as a population, not 323 independent examples
Cross-modal edges (US_i → Mammo_j) capture cross-modal similarity patterns invisible in within-modal graphs
Dynamic graph — re-computed each epoch using improving representations (curriculum learning effect)
Directly exploits BCMID's small-N as an advantage — 323 nodes is a tractable graph; larger datasets would be computationally prohibitive
